<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Eugene of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1663">1663</a>–<a href="/years/1736">1736</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Eugene of Savoy · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Eugene of Savoy was a Paris-born general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> service and one of the greatest commanders of his age. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Zenta in 1697, shared victory with Marlborough at Blenheim in 1704 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, and in the war of 1716 to 1718 won at Petrovaradin, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeswar</a> and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. His campaigns secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> for the Habsburg monarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Prince Eugene of Savoy" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacob van Schuppen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prinz_Eugene_of_Savoy.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eugene was born at the Hôtel de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 18 October 1663, the youngest of five sons of Eugene Maurice, Count of Soissons, a French general officer, and Olympia Mancini, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a>. From the age of ten he was brought up for a career in the church, but at 19 he chose a military career. Because of his poor physique and possibly a scandal involving his mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> refused him a commission in the French army and forbade him to enlist elsewhere. Embittered, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and entered the service of Emperor Leopold I, in whose army his elder brother Louis Julius had just fallen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Eugene arrived at Leopold's camp in mid-August 1683, when the Ottoman army was besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and distinguished himself there at the age of 20. He commanded troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> in 1686 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1688 and was a field marshal by 25. As commander-in-chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zenta">Battle of Zenta</a> in 1697, which ended the Ottoman threat for nearly twenty years. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> he won victories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1706, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Duke of Marlborough</a> won at Blenheim in 1704, Oudenaarde in 1708 and Malplaquet in 1709.</p><p>In 1716 Charles VI sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> against the Turks. With about 70,000 men he attacked the Grand Vizier Damat Ali Pasha's army of some 200,000 on 5 August at Petrovaradin; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">Vizier</a> died of wounds. Eugene took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1717 and secured peace in 1718. As president of the Imperial War Council he later led a defensive campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession">War of the Polish Succession</a>, hampered by poor troops and a shortage of funds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eugene returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in October 1735, weak and unwell, and died in his sleep in the night of 20-21 April 1736. His heart was buried with his ancestors in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-superga">Basilica of Superga</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and his body in St Stephen's Cathedral. He never married. A patron of the arts, he built the Stadtpalais in Vienna and, with the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-lukas-von-hildebrandt">Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palaces including the Belvedere. Napoleon counted him among the eight great commanders of history, though later military critics have disagreed. An equestrian statue in Vienna calls him the wise counsellor of three emperors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince Eugene of Savoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Amadeus II fought both sides in the wars of Louis XIV: defeated by the French at Staffarda in 1690 and Marsaglia in 1693, he made a separate peace in 1696 that returned Pinerolo, and in 1706 he and <b>Prince Eugene of Savoy</b> broke the French Siege of Turin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Holy League retook Buda in 1686, and <b>Prince Eugene of Savoy</b> destroyed the Ottoman army at the Battle of Zenta in 1697, leading to the Treaty of Karlowitz in 1699.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duke of Marlborough and <b>Prince Eugene of Savoy</b> destroyed a Franco-Bavarian army at Blenheim.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of the Spanish Succession, French forces under Marshal Villars won a victory at Denain over the Dutch and Austrian forces of <b>Prince Eugene of Savoy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-24">24 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Prince Eugene of Savoy</b> surprised the Ottoman army of Sultan Mustafa II as it crossed the Tisza at Zenta and destroyed it, killing tens of thousands, including the grand vizier.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-11">11 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Prince Eugene of Savoy</b> captured Temeswar from the Ottomans in 1716, and the Treaty of Passarowitz in 1718 confirmed Habsburg possession of the Banat between the Mureș, Tisza and Danube.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-temeswar">Banat of Temeswar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1710" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Prince Eugene of Savoy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1710">What was here in 1710? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
